  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: \nStorm chances stay with us into the weekend with best chances\nin the mountains. Some severe/flooding possible mainly through\nthis evening and again late Wednesday into Thursday morning.\n\nJuicy airmass in place and have kept flood watch as is, with\nmore concern over our WV counties, but over time, southward\nshift in the path of storms would impact the NC mountains. \n\nSo far storms have been moving fast enough with limited\ntraining/repeat storms to keep flood threat down, but another\ncluster of storms in WV early this afternoon will likely pose\nsome localized flooding problems through late afternoon.\n\nSevere storms will be possible this afternoon with greater\ninstability from far southwest VA into NC where sunshine was\nmore plentiful to allow destablization, though better shear is\nfurther north.\n\nThe MCS moving into our SE WV counties early this afternoon is \nnot as unstable, thanks to earlier convection and clouds and \nconsiderable uncertainty exists regarding this system's \nlongevity. Nevertheless, sufficient flow aloft and mesoscale \norganization will maintain a risk of damaging wind gusts. \n\nYet another upstream MCS over the lower Great Lakes could ride\nsoutheast into the area this evening. Models vary on how this\ntranspires, but forecast Corfidi vectors point to the same\ngeneral path into eastern Ohio, then southeast into WV/SW VA.\nSome models are having it turn more south through KY while\nothers split it and have one piece going across PA/MD.\nConfidence is lower this evening on pops but have seen enough\ntrends/pattern recognition to at least have confidence of likely\npops in the north along I-64. then southwest into WV/far SW VA\nwith less chances in NC. But as can be the case with MCS's, they\ncan hold together, even in a weakened state to bring measurable\nprecip to most of the area.\n\nModel trends have backed off on coverage of storms Wednesday,\nbut am not confident in this, but will lean drier as pattern\nremains the same though upstream vorts appear weaker.\n\nStill, strong to severe storms will be possible again Wednesday\nafternoon/evening. The evolution of upstream convection will \nhave a considerable influence on the position of the primary \nsurface front and any organized severe threat across parts of \nthe region on Wednesday. At least isolated storm development \nwill be possible near the front and also across favored terrain \nareas, with modest west- northwesterly flow aloft supporting \npotential for outflow-driven cells/clusters with a threat of \nwind damage. Any features favorably timed with the diurnal cycle\n(such as a persistent storm cluster or MCV) could result in a \ngreater coverage of wind-damage potential, but confidence is \nlow.\n\nWe keep a pattern of a front stalled or slowly sinking south\ninto the end of the week. Maybe by the weekend we dry out some,\nbut confidence is not high enough to pull chances of storms.",
